# @corpus current
# THE APPLICATION LADDER — gap[110], c0 §43 `law.brace`, §44 `apply.edge` /
# `law.pack.shape`. Owner ruling 2026-08-09, reconciled 2026-08-13
# (`law.injection.only` retired the `@{ … }` rung).
#
# ONE semantic case — the point (3.0, 4.0) — through every live rung of the
# ladder, most explicit to most inferred, in ONE file so no rung can drift
# without the others noticing:
#
#     q = point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }      rung 0  descriptor STATED
#     p: point = { x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }    rung 1  descriptor = the DEMANDED one
#     m = make(3.0, 4.0)                 rung 2  descriptor = the RESULT demand
#
# gap[110] measured the ladder INVERTED on 2026-08-09: only rung 0 worked,
# rung 1 broke the C on read, and rung 2 did not even bind. This fixture is
# the pin on the repaired state: every rung answers, and the answers AGREE —
# 3 three times, then 4 three times. A single divergent digit is a rung
# constructing something other than the point the others constructed.
#
# The DIAGNOSTIC half of the parity is the negative twin
# `examples/compile_fail/expect_apply_label.id`: rung 1 refuses a label the
# descriptor has no field for with the same law rung 0 already enforced
# (`examples/compile_fail/apply_one_label.id`). Read the twins together —
# either alone passes under a compiler that had picked one rung to believe.
#
# `{…}` stays semantically neutral throughout: nothing here makes a brace mean
# "construct"; the subject arrives from the stated name at rung 0 and from
# demand at rungs 1 and 2, exactly as PACK and EXPECT-APPLY rule.

point: { x: f64, y: f64 }

make: point = (a: f64, b: f64)
    { a, b }

main = ()
    q = point{ x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }
    p: point = { x = 3.0, y = 4.0 }
    m = make(3.0, 4.0)
    print(q.x)
    print(p.x)
    print(m.x)
    print(q.y)
    print(p.y)
    print(m.y)
